Yes, if you want this to be correct with nonblocking sockets, 
you should switch to EPOLLOUT when there is pending response data to write.

The usual flow is:

build the response string after parsing the request
store it in the connection state
add EPOLLOUT to that client’s epoll events
in the writable handler, call send()
if send() writes only part of the buffer, keep the rest and wait for the next EPOLLOUT
once everything is sent, remove EPOLLOUT and go back to EPOLLIN
So not “use EPOLLOUT before every send” in the literal sense, but “register EPOLLOUT when you have something to send and finish sending in the write event.” That is the proper design for your current nonblocking setup.
